@government
go-to: menu

Although the "Republic of China," as it is recognized by foreign powers, is notionally ruled by a central government based in Beijing, in practice this government's authority is extremely weak, and is torn between ever-dividing factions of self-interested militarists and bureacrats who struggle to exert control over any area not under the direct occupation of their rapacious armies. Over the rest of the nation, authority rests in the hands of warlords, generals, and local leaders. There are surely even remote corners of the nation that are not even aware the old Qing dynasty fell at all!

The exception to this lawlessness lies here in the Revolutionary Nationalist Government based out of [? if started != 1: Guangzhou?][? if current_capital == "guangzhou": Guangzhou?][? if current_capital == "wuhan": Wuhan?][? if current_capital == "nanjing": Nanjing?]. It is here that the Chinese Nationalist Party, the Kuomintang, has launched its long awaited campaign to reunify the nation under the principles of Sun Yat-sen, in the name of prosperity for all and against foreign imperialism. While our militaries march northward, in our base areas we are hard at work constructing a modern and advanced Chinese state.


@china_timeline
go-to: menu

August 20, 1905 - The Revolutionary Alliance (Tongmenghui) is founded in Tokyo, Japan

October 10, 1911 - An uprising begins in the Wuchang garrison, quickly spreading in the coming weeks to neighboring provinces

December 29, 1911 - Sun Yat-sen is elected president by the provisional parliament in Nanjing

January 1, 1912 - The Republic of China is declared

February 12, 1912 - The Xuantong Emperor abdicates





